# DRY + SOLID

**DRY:** One authoritative implementation per business rule. Search for existing helpers, tokens, types and components before adding code. Extract repeated behavior when it is a stable, meaningful concept; do not over-abstract incidental repetition.

**S — Single responsibility:** Each module/component has one clear reason to change. Split data access, business behavior and UI presentation when it improves clarity.

**O — Open/closed:** Prefer extension through typed composition and configuration over editing every caller. Avoid plugin systems for hypothetical extension points.

**L — Liskov substitution:** Implementations sharing an interface must preserve its contract, including errors, loading and data invariants.

**I — Interface segregation:** Small task-oriented types beat huge service interfaces. Expose only what callers require.

**D — Dependency inversion:** Domain logic depends on interfaces and pure functions. Stripe, Supabase and Google Calendar adapters stay at integration boundaries.

Every abstraction needs an actual second use case or clear module boundary. Simplicity wins.
